1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,346
Every product you've evaluated this year claims to have an agent in it now.

2
00:00:04,346 --> 00:00:05,199
Some of them do.

3
00:00:05,199 --> 00:00:09,675
Most of them are a fixed sequence of steps someone designed in advance, with a model

4
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:12,393
filling in text at one or two points along the way.

5
00:00:12,393 --> 00:00:16,762
Both get called the same word, and the difference between them is exactly what you

6
00:00:16,762 --> 00:00:17,988
need to be able to see.

7
00:00:17,988 --> 00:00:18,840
Here's the test.

8
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:23,210
Who decides what happens next: the person who built the system, in advance, or the

9
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:26,034
model, in the moment, based on what it just observed.

10
00:00:26,034 --> 00:00:27,313
A workflow is the first.

11
00:00:27,313 --> 00:00:28,538
An agent is the second.

12
00:00:28,538 --> 00:00:32,748
The distinction is worth this much attention because you'll be asked to approve

13
00:00:32,748 --> 00:00:36,798
budget for one, scope one, or explain to legal what one is allowed to touch.

14
00:00:36,798 --> 00:00:41,221
Answering any of those well depends on knowing which system you're actually looking

15
00:00:41,221 --> 00:00:43,352
at, not which word is on the pitch deck.

16
00:00:43,902 --> 00:00:45,734
That loop is the entire mechanism.

17
00:00:45,734 --> 00:00:50,100
Look at the current state, decide what to do about it, take that action through a

18
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:54,411
tool, look at what happened, and decide again, until the model itself judges the

19
00:00:54,411 --> 00:00:55,166
task finished.

20
00:00:55,716 --> 00:01:00,326
Take the support-ticket summariser from Section two, and push it one step further

21
00:01:00,326 --> 00:01:01,578
than it's gone so far.

22
00:01:01,578 --> 00:01:06,358
Right now it only drafts a summary, the kind that dropped Ticket 4471's deadline and

23
00:01:06,358 --> 00:01:09,090
left a human to catch the double charge by hand.

24
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:10,740
Suppose it could act instead.

25
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:15,463
An agent observes a refund request landing in that same queue, decides it needs the

26
00:01:15,463 --> 00:01:19,902
order history, calls a tool to pull it, observes what came back, and only then

27
00:01:19,902 --> 00:01:24,000
decides whether this qualifies for an automatic refund or needs a human.

28
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,699
That decision genuinely depends on what the order history showed.

29
00:01:27,699 --> 00:01:32,138
Nobody wrote "always approve" or "always escalate" into the system in advance.

30
00:01:32,138 --> 00:01:35,325
The model decided, in the moment, from what it observed.

31
00:01:35,325 --> 00:01:37,373
Say it decides the refund qualifies.

32
00:01:37,373 --> 00:01:40,219
That's not the end of the loop, it's the next lap.

33
00:01:40,219 --> 00:01:44,942
It calls a tool to issue the refund, observes that the payment system confirmed it,

34
00:01:44,942 --> 00:01:49,666
and decides again: does this specific customer's pattern, three refunds in a month,

35
00:01:49,666 --> 00:01:53,991
say, need a fraud flag before it closes the case, or is a confirmation email

36
00:01:53,991 --> 00:01:54,958
genuinely enough.

37
00:01:54,958 --> 00:01:59,568
Each lap through that loop is a fresh decision made from what the last one turned

38
00:01:59,568 --> 00:02:02,243
up, not a step ticked off a predetermined list.

39
00:02:02,793 --> 00:02:06,404
Notice both columns can produce an identical-looking refund email.

40
00:02:06,404 --> 00:02:10,673
The difference is invisible in the output and everything in how the system got

41
00:02:10,673 --> 00:02:11,001
there.

42
00:02:11,001 --> 00:02:13,628
Run the same test on anything you're evaluating.

43
00:02:13,628 --> 00:02:17,896
Ask what happens if the order history comes back unusual: a duplicate order, a

44
00:02:17,896 --> 00:02:22,110
customer with three prior refunds this month, an item that was never actually

45
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:22,547
shipped.

46
00:02:22,547 --> 00:02:26,761
A workflow either has no branch for that, or someone had to anticipate it and

47
00:02:26,761 --> 00:02:27,691
hard-code a rule.

48
00:02:27,691 --> 00:02:32,014
An agent decides, because deciding what to do next from what it just saw is the

49
00:02:32,014 --> 00:02:34,860
entire mechanism, not a special case bolted onto it.

50
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:39,238
Vendors have every incentive to blur this, because agent is the word that raises

51
00:02:39,238 --> 00:02:43,287
money and workflow is the word that sounds like the thing you already had.

52
00:02:43,287 --> 00:02:47,391
Reading a product's own documentation for the phrase "supported actions" or

53
00:02:47,391 --> 00:02:51,878
"configured steps" is usually the fastest tell: that's workflow language, whatever

54
00:02:51,878 --> 00:02:53,411
the marketing page calls it.

55
00:02:53,961 --> 00:02:58,477
Say the honest caveat now, because the next few lectures spend real time on exactly

56
00:02:58,477 --> 00:02:59,022
this cost.

57
00:02:59,022 --> 00:03:00,817
Why is a workflow so predictable?

58
00:03:00,817 --> 00:03:03,593
Because nobody left room for it to decide anything.

59
00:03:03,593 --> 00:03:07,892
An agent's whole value is that room, and that same room is where it goes wrong:

60
00:03:07,892 --> 00:03:12,463
approves a refund it shouldn't have, calls the wrong tool, decides it's done when it

61
00:03:12,463 --> 00:03:12,790
isn't.

62
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:17,252
This section's next lecture puts an actual number on how fast that risk compounds.

63
00:03:17,252 --> 00:03:21,660
For now, just hold the trade plainly: more autonomy, more reach, and more ways to

64
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:22,367
fail quietly.

65
00:03:22,367 --> 00:03:26,068
This is also why the refund example keeps returning to money moving.

66
00:03:26,068 --> 00:03:30,421
Blast radius scales with what the agent's tools can actually touch, not with how

67
00:03:30,421 --> 00:03:32,435
clever its reasoning looks in a demo.

68
00:03:32,435 --> 00:03:36,843
A five-step agent that only drafts text is a different risk profile entirely from

69
00:03:36,843 --> 00:03:41,251
one that issues refunds, and this section keeps that distinction live throughout.

70
00:03:41,801 --> 00:03:46,343
That's the definition worth carrying past this lecture, and the second half is doing

71
00:03:46,343 --> 00:03:47,748
as much work as the first.

72
00:03:47,748 --> 00:03:52,128
Not a fixed sequence with a model generating text at one step, because that's the

73
00:03:52,128 --> 00:03:54,994
version most "agent" marketing is quietly describing.

74
00:03:54,994 --> 00:03:57,427
The exercise is genuinely worth five minutes.

75
00:03:57,427 --> 00:04:01,050
Most products fail it honestly, and that's not a criticism of them.

76
00:04:01,050 --> 00:04:03,645
A well-built workflow is often the right answer.

77
00:04:03,645 --> 00:04:07,917
It just isn't an agent, and knowing which one you're actually looking at is the

78
00:04:07,917 --> 00:04:09,430
whole point of this lecture.

79
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:13,972
Keep the test handy past today, because you'll need it in rooms where nobody else in

80
00:04:13,972 --> 00:04:16,351
the conversation is drawing the distinction.

81
00:04:16,351 --> 00:04:20,731
A vendor demo, a roadmap review, an engineer proposing "an agent" for something a

82
00:04:20,731 --> 00:04:23,543
workflow would do just as well and far more cheaply.

83
00:04:23,543 --> 00:04:27,922
Being the person who asks the one question that actually settles it is worth more

84
00:04:27,922 --> 00:04:29,112
than knowing the term.

85
00:04:29,662 --> 00:04:31,267
One thing before you move on.

86
00:04:31,267 --> 00:04:35,084
Run the test for real, on a specific product, not a hypothetical one.

87
00:04:35,084 --> 00:04:39,732
Who decided what happens next: its builder, in advance, or the model, in the moment.

88
00:04:39,732 --> 00:04:44,047
Then name one point where that decision could go wrong, concretely, not in the

89
00:04:44,047 --> 00:04:44,545
abstract.

90
00:04:44,545 --> 00:04:47,920
"It might misunderstand the customer" is too vague to act on.

91
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:52,457
"It might approve a refund for an item that was already returned once" is the kind

92
00:04:52,457 --> 00:04:55,113
of finding that actually changes what you build.

93
00:04:55,113 --> 00:04:59,705
Next lecture gives you the tools and connections that let an agent reach beyond its

94
00:04:59,705 --> 00:05:04,187
own output into your real systems, and the lecture after that gives you the maths

95
00:05:04,187 --> 00:05:06,179
for exactly the risk you just named.
